    "updated_at": "2026-11-22T19:29:27Z",
    "body": "Looks already fixed on upstream/main 6e641dac7: the test below passes without a change. Suggest closing; the maintainer decides.\n\nWhat the test does (`crates/motion/src/drilling/tests_tilted.rs`, branch `mkinney:issue/3554-tilted-hole-pin`, commit 418e88034):\n\n- `tilted_wall_builder_at_zero_tilt_is_a_z_hole`: the mesh builder at 0 degrees gives one Z hole. This shows that the fixture is correct.\n- `tilted_holes_in_a_plate_are_not_drilled_along_z`: a 10 mm plate has one hole tilted 2 degrees and one tilted 5 degrees. `recognize` finds no Z hole. `recognize_cross` finds no cross hole. The test also tries 1, 2 and 8 bands and -2 degrees. All give no Z hole.\n- `a_tilt_the_fit_accepts_moves_no_wall_past_fit_tol`: thin walls (0.3 mm to 3 mm) with a small tilt. When the fit accepts one as a Z hole, each wall vertex is within `FIT_TOL` (0.02 mm) of the found circle.\n\nWhy the hypothesis is false:\n\n- The circle fit in `classify` (`recognize.rs`) uses all wall vertices, from the top and the bottom of the hole.\n- The tilt moves the section centre by depth x tan(angle). For 2 degrees over 10 mm, this is 0.35 mm. The fit then has a residual near 0.17 mm. This is far above `FIT_TOL`, so the fit refuses the wall.\n- The ellipse error (about 0.0006 r) is not the controlling term. The axial slide is.\n- A short tilted hole can pass. Then `FIT_TOL` limits the wall movement to 0.02 mm. This is the same tolerance that each tessellated hole has.\n\nMutation: with `FIT_TOL` set to 0.5, `tilted_holes_in_a_plate_are_not_drilled_along_z` fails. With 0.02 it passes. The pin guards the fit.\n\nThe fit residual check is on main since 2458afc69 / 5f9294fb3 (M4.1, M5.0.1). No later commit changed it for this case.\n\nThe issue asks to keep the test as a pin. The branch above has it. A PR can take it if the maintainer wants it. A tilted hole is dropped with no named message. A named skip is a separate enhancement, not a gouge.\n\nI release my claim.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
